Ember Throne credits

Characters: built from CC0 bases by this repository's own tools
  sources     Quaternius, "Universal Base Characters" (Standard, free) and "Modular Character Outfits - Fantasy"
              (Standard, free), CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication):
                https://quaternius.com/packs/universalbasecharacters.html
                https://quaternius.com/packs/modularcharacteroutfitsfantasy.html
                https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
              The files used (the ranger and peasant outfits, the two base bodies' heads, eyes and brows, the hair
              styles), with their textures resized, are in tools/chars/cc0 with the packs' licence notes
              (LICENSE-base.txt, LICENSE-outfits.txt). CC0 asks for nothing; the credit is given anyway.
  changes     tools/chars/blender/build_chars.py, cast.py, dress.py (run headless in Blender; Blender is a tool, its
              output is ours): each figure posed to the game's rest, sized and bulked, its head and hands enlarged,
              recoloured, its skin baked into one atlas; the plate, helmets, crowns, horns, robes, coats, capes, masks
              and bracers are generated there. tools/chars/make-characters.mjs (copied from more-cartwheel-examples'
              brawler, with this game's clips: the sword combo, the heavy spin, the dive roll, the sidesteps, the
              kneel at a shrine, the flask, the push, the roar) puts them on the game's skeleton with its clips.
              tools/chars/build-cast.sh runs both. (make-characters.mjs now writes each figure on the full source skeleton,
              every spine, shoulder, finger and toe joint, with no clips of its own; the motion below is retargeted onto it.)

Motion: the characters' clips, from CC0 motion libraries on the same rig as the bases
  sources     Quaternius, "Universal Animation Library" (Standard, free) and "Universal Animation Library 2" (Standard,
              free), CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication):
                https://quaternius.com/packs/universalanimationlibrary.html
                https://opengameart.org/content/universal-animation-library
                https://opengameart.org/content/universal-animation-library-2
                https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
              The libraries' licence notes are in tools/chars/cc0/ual (LICENSE-ual1.txt, LICENSE-ual2.txt); the files
              are fetched and checked by tools/chars/fetch-anims.sh. CC0 asks for nothing; the credit is given anyway.
  changes     tools/chars/blender/build_anims.py (run headless in Blender) picks the clips the game plays (the stands, the
              walk, the jog, the three-cut sword combo with its recoveries, the dive roll, the hits, the knock-down and
              get-up, the kneel and rest, the drink, the chest, the push, a chop, an overhead throw, a lunge, a shield
              dash, a hook), cuts, joins and retimes them, takes their root travel out, and makes the clips the libraries
              lack from their motion: the sidesteps (the walk turned at the hips, the chest kept forward), the back walk
              (the walk run backwards), the spinning heavy (the wide cut whipped round a full turn, the hips leading),
              a boss's three-turn lariat, the back step (the library's hop, quick), and a boss's roar (keyed on the rig
              with the joints staggered). The result is assets/anim/ual.glb (with the libraries' mannequin to carry the
              rig); the engine's packer retargets it onto each figure (assets/models/*.glb.cwimport).

Ground: the terrain's eight layers, from CC0 photo-scanned PBR sets (colour, normal, roughness, height, occlusion)
  sources     Poly Haven, CC0 1.0 Universal (https://polyhaven.com/license):
                dry_ground_rocks      (the paths)          https://polyhaven.com/a/dry_ground_rocks
                sandstone_cracks      (the canyon floor)   https://polyhaven.com/a/sandstone_cracks
                rock_face             (cliffs and rock)    https://polyhaven.com/a/rock_face
                mud_forest            (the marsh, moss)    https://polyhaven.com/a/mud_forest
                cobblestone_floor_04  (paving)             https://polyhaven.com/a/cobblestone_floor_04
                burned_ground_01      (the keep's hill)    https://polyhaven.com/a/burned_ground_01
              ambientCG, CC0 1.0 Universal (https://docs.ambientcg.com/license/):
                Grass004              (grass)              https://ambientcg.com/view?id=Grass004
                Snow006               (the heights)        https://ambientcg.com/view?id=Snow006
              CC0 asks for nothing; the credit is given anyway.
  changes     tools/make-ground.mjs fetches them, heals any faint step at their edges, takes their tile-sized patches
              out, grades each to its region's palette and packs them as the engine's layers (albedo and height, normal
              and roughness, 1024 square), checking that each one tiles.

Voices: every line spoken by Kokoro-82M v1.0 (hexgrad, Apache-2.0) in its ONNX build (onnx-community/Kokoro-82M-v1.0-ONNX,
  Apache-2.0), voices bf_isabella, af_bella, bm_george, bf_emma, am_onyx, bm_lewis and am_adam from the same release,
  run by kokoro-js (Apache-2.0) through the engine's tools/speech-bake.mjs (tools/story.sh). The model is a tool; only
  the audio it spoke ships (assets/story/ember.cwspeech).
Combat ideas: the parry window, the special meter and its finisher, the assist call and the boss ladder follow keyholder
  (github.com/virgilvox/keyholder, MIT); the code here is this game's own.

Everything else in this game is generated by this repository's own code:
  story       story/ember.story (the script), tale.c (scenes, barks, the people at the shrines, faces, shots)
  world       world.c (the terrain's heights and paint, every building, stair, gate and door, all from tables at
              start-up) and tools/make-world-assets.mjs (the building textures and every prop:
              weapons, keys, the heart vessel, chests, shrines, trees, rocks, braziers, pillars, banners)
  sound       fx.c and main.c (every sound and the music are computed)
  fonts       the engine's built-in UI font (see the engine's NOTICE)
  art         tools/capture-art.sh and tools/make-art.mjs (the launcher art from the game's own frames; the name set in
              DejaVu Serif Bold, the system's free font, Bitstream Vera licence)

Third-party assets: the CC0 character, motion and ground sources above; the launcher art's lettering uses the DejaVu Serif Bold font.
